  "type": "answer",
  "content": "Assumption: \"slow\" means the tiered VMs on esxi-pay-05, and the same pattern on other hosts is unverified. The cause is a working set that no longer fits DRAM on esxi-pay-05, so tiered VMs wait on NVMe fetches. Act now by moving load off that host.\n\n**Mechanism**\n- Active memory is 790 GB, 77% of DRAM. Above 75% means significant loss is expected (is-memory-tiering-slowing-host, step 2).\n- NVMe reads stayed high an hour apart (380 then 360 MBps). That is a sustained working set beyond DRAM, not a weekend warm-up (step 3).\n- NVMe read latency of 290 µs is in the \"potential issues\" band (step 4).\n- sql-pay-03's full 256 GB reservation shrinks the DRAM left for the 34 tiered VMs (step 7). That explains why pay-05 is worse than pay-07 and pay-08, which hold no SQL VM.\n\n**Premises that fail**\n- pay-api-07's 0.2% Memory Contention does not rule out tiering. That counter is unaware of NVMe fetches. Its NVMe wait is 6 × 256 × 0.00029 ÷ 8 = 5.57% (cluster-performance), which matches VM Performance at 17%.\n- \"Capacity Remaining (%)\" invites reading it as capacity free now. It is really a 3-Day Forecast Capacity Remaining (%) from last Wednesday, taken before the Thursday change. The KB does not say whether it reads active memory against DRAM. Cluster Risk at 66% is the current reading (cluster-risk).\n- The DBA's \"no wait\" fits a VM with a full reservation. It does not clear sql-pay-03's effect on its neighbours.\n\n**Do now**\n1. Rank the VMs on esxi-pay-05 by NVMe read bandwidth (step 5).\n2. vMotion the heaviest readers to esxi-pay-07 and esxi-pay-08, after confirming their DRAM headroom.\n3. Check the active memory of hosts 01–04 and 06 before using them.\n\n**Check next**\n- The change calendar for Tue 23:25 and Thu 23:35.\n- Migration history.\n- The teller app VMs' hosts.\n\nNot settled by the knowledge base: whether the Tuesday and Thursday changes caused this, and why DRS did not rebalance.",
