  "content": "Neither change alone caused it; the two together did. Do not roll back CHG-2 as the first remedy. Rebalance DRAM inside the cluster now.\n\nMechanism\n- **CHG-1 (Tue 23:25):** each SQL VM now holds 256 GB of DRAM, fully reserved and not tiered. Each full reservation shrinks the DRAM left for the tiered VMs (is-memory-tiering-slowing-host, step 7). On esxi-pay-05, sql-pay-03 leaves at most 768 GB of DRAM for 34 tiered VMs, before hypervisor overhead.\n- **CHG-2 (Thu 23:35):** 40 tiered VMs arrived. On the worst host, the overcommit ratio rose from 1.07 to 1.23 and DRAM Consumed passed 80%, so tiering started.\n- **The weekend:** cold pages moved to NVMe (writes up, reads near 0).\n- **Tuesday morning:** active memory is 790 GB, 77% of DRAM, above the 75% point where significant loss is expected. NVMe reads stayed high an hour apart, so this is a sustained working set, not a warm-up (step 3).\n\nThe two changes split DRAM between reserved SQL and tiered VMs. Neither alone shows this.\n\n**The approval check:** \"Capacity Remaining (%)\" invites reading it as room free now. It fails twice:\n- It is a 3-day projection of utilization.\n- VCF Operations counts the NVMe tier as DRAM, so supply looked doubled (capacity-forecast).\n\nThe better model is 3-Day Forecast Capacity Remaining (%). On a tiered cluster, also read active memory against DRAM and Cluster Risk.\n\nDo now\n1. vMotion the heaviest NVMe readers off esxi-pay-05 (and any host with a SQL VM) to esxi-pay-07 and esxi-pay-08 (44% and 41% active).\n2. Check where the six SQL VMs sit. Keep at most one per host (6 SQL VMs, 8 hosts).\n3. Recheck active memory and NVMe reads after each move.\n\nNot settled by the knowledge base: whether the old cluster can take the 40 VMs back, why DRS did not rebalance, and where the teller VMs run.",
